Authoritative graphics pipeline state
====================================

Source proposal: docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.Proposal.md
Approval: user approved the proposal on 2026-10-03. Implementation has not started.
Project index: project.todo / Performance / PipelineState.

Goal and execution contract
  Establish explicit state ownership for VGE-owned graphics draws, preserving shader activation,
  framebuffer attachment ownership, engine restoration, and redundant native-call suppression.
  The proposal is authoritative. This plan sequences implementation; it does not broaden scope.
  New types and migrated types use responsibility-based names without Gl/GL/Gpu prefixes.
  Existing source names below locate current implementations, not prescribed final names.
  Keep files focused, dependencies directional, and GraphicsCommandContext a thin composition root.
  Follow repository XML comments, visibility regions, and nontrivial-method comment conventions.
  Delegate all builds and tests to subagents. Record commands, results, failures and limitations.
  Never launch Vintage Story. Live game/RenderDoc evidence is supplied by the user.
  Keep tasks unchecked until their stated verification is complete. Distinguish source review,
  unit/headless integration evidence, live rendering evidence, and measured performance.
  Implementation evidence belongs in docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.md (to be created).

Scope boundaries
  Preserve existing resource hierarchies, disposal queues, shader reflection/preparation, generated
  shader inputs, compute submission, and FBO borrowing/owning contracts.
  Retain explicit partial overrides at documented compatibility boundaries.
  Resource hazards, barriers, resolves, blits and readback remain explicit operations.
  Renderbuffer binding is resource-operation state, not graphics pipeline identity.
  No render graph, deferred command recording, multithreaded GL submission or additional backend.
  Pipeline interning, prewarming and asynchronous preparation remain follow-up capabilities.
  No blanket engine-wide authority claim or removal of all invalidation/restoration scopes.
  Rename types as they migrate; a repository-wide unrelated resource rename is outside this plan.

Approved sequencing exception — engine-boundary restoration (2026-10-05)
  Source: docs/Rendering.EngineBoundaryRestoration.Proposal.md, approved by the user.
  Execution plan: docs/Rendering.EngineBoundaryRestoration.todo.
  Bring forward the shared StateCache restoration mechanism and pre-overlay refraction adapter
  before complete graphics submission and the normal production-consumer migration sequence.
  First establish the affected boundary inventory, context policy, state coverage and validation.
  This permits the bounded restoration work without completing all of Phases 1–2; broader
  Phase 3 coverage and the remaining pipeline/submission prerequisites retain their dependencies.
  Reuse this mechanism when GraphicsCommandContext and complete pipelines are introduced.
  Keep all completion markers unchecked until their full contracts and verification are satisfied.
  Inventory and categorized cache foundation are complete under the linked restoration plan.
  Boundary snapshots/restoration and consumer integration remain pending; no parent phase is complete.

Phase 1: Establish state and consumer contracts
  Dependencies: approved proposal.
  Outcome: explicit supported state inventory and migration boundaries that later work can implement.

  ☐ Inventory graphics consumers and native/engine mutation paths.
    ☐ Locate descriptor application, framebuffer blend policy, raw GL changes, shader activation,
      MeshRef/VAO draws, tessellation setup, and capture/invalidation scopes.
    ☐ Classify every consumer as VGE-owned or engine-owned; record required adapters and migration order.
    ☐ Classify mutation paths as observed, explicitly restored or unknown, including existing Harmony hooks.
    ☐ Audit renderbuffer binding/scope coverage without adding it to the PSO key.
  ☐ Resolve and document the proposal's open design choices before their dependent implementations.
    ☐ Specify supported state and defaults: depth/stencil, rasterizer, indexed blending, sampling,
      framebuffer sRGB, clipping/depth conventions, restart, patch size and shader-controlled point size.
    ☐ Declare viewport/scissor, stencil reference and blend constant dynamic where applicable;
      choose depth-range ownership and line-width/point-size policy from actual consumers.
    ☐ Define vertex layout and geometry adapters for MeshRef and VGE VAOs.
    ☐ Define exact normalized format/sample matching, sparse routing and intentionally discarded outputs.
    ☐ Define wrapped/default framebuffer metadata providers and refresh boundaries.
    ☐ Establish shader executable generation and context generation identity sources.
    ☐ Specify per-boundary engine restoration sets and known-state versus capture policy.
  ☐ Create the implementation evidence document with a consumer/state coverage matrix and source links.
    ☐ Record deterministic direct-lighting reference cases and comparison methodology before migration.
    ☐ Record unsupported features as rejected or excluded by a verified boundary, never ambient defaults.
  ☐ Completion gate: every open choice has a documented decision for the first consumer and a defined
    resolution prerequisite for remaining consumers; scope and evidence categories are explicit.

Phase 2: Complete immutable descriptions
  Dependencies: Phase 1 state, layout, target and naming decisions.
  Outcome: complete pipeline inputs with stable structural identity and release-build validation.

  ☐ Introduce GraphicsPipelineDesc and focused state value types.
    ☐ Represent shader variant/layout identity, vertex layout, topology, target signature and dynamics.
    ☐ Cover rasterizer cull/mode/winding, polygon mode, bias, clamp/clip, discard and provoking convention.
    ☐ Cover depth comparison/write policy and front/back stencil comparisons, operations and masks.
    ☐ Cover per-output blend enables, RGB/alpha factors/equations and write masks.
    ☐ Cover supported multisample, mask/coverage, alpha-to-coverage and sRGB settings.
    ☐ Include restart and tessellation control-point configuration and other inventoried relevant state.
  ☐ Resolve construction omissions against explicit defaults; never inherit live GL state.
    ☐ Require explicit dynamic declarations; canonicalize inactive values without losing re-enable behavior.
    ☐ Separate partial override identity from complete descriptions; retain stable legacy mask numbering.
  ☐ Implement deep immutability, structural equality and hashing.
    ☐ Include every behavior-affecting field; exclude debug labels, actual FBO IDs and dimensions.
    ☐ Separate reusable description identity from live executable/context realization identity.
    ☐ Reject invalid combinations and unsupported capabilities in all builds.
  ☐ Delegate focused unit tests in Debug and Release for immutability, equivalent copied arrays,
    field-sensitive identity, defaults, dynamic requirements and invalid combinations.
  ☐ Completion gate: published descriptions cannot be mutated; supported state has explicit ownership;
    complete and partial descriptions cannot be accidentally interchanged.

Phase 3: Context-aware state application and engine boundaries
  Dependencies: Phases 1–2.
  Outcome: complete descriptions can be applied independently of prior state and safely restored.

  ☐ Extend and migrate GlStateCache to StateCache, updating references atomically with the rename.
    ☐ Implement all supported static/dynamic transitions while preserving existing binding caches.
    ☐ Handle global/indexed blend interactions, write masks and disabled-to-enabled transitions.
    ☐ Associate knowledge with context generation; reset on replacement/loss/switch as required.
    ☐ Invalidate affected resource bindings on retirement and native-name reuse; preserve VAO-aware EBO tracking.
  ☐ Implement focused engine boundary adapters for the first consumer and shared mechanisms.
    ☐ Capture or obtain required incoming state; restore native state and engine shader ownership.
    ☐ Observe successful known engine mutations without duplicate native calls or recursive hooks.
    ☐ Retain invalidation for unobserved paths; document explicit external-operation boundaries.
    ☐ Ensure exception paths restore the boundary and leave cache knowledge truthful.
  ☐ Add bounded instrumentation for state calls, queries and draw submission verification.
  ☐ Delegate headless transition tests after deliberately hostile prior state and A-to-B-to-A changes.
    ☐ Verify indexed blend/masks, sampling, stencil and rasterizer results, not only cached fields.
    ☐ Verify redundant-call suppression, context replacement, retirement and name reuse.
    ☐ Verify boundary restoration and unknown external mutation handling on success and exceptions.
  ☐ Completion gate: first-consumer state application and restoration are proven before production
    migration; unverified engine paths remain explicitly outside authority.

Phase 4: Prepared graphics pipelines and executable compatibility
  Dependencies: Phases 1–3, including executable/context identities.
  Outcome: validated GraphicsPipeline realizations usable by submission without per-draw reflection.

  ☐ Compose existing shader readiness, GpuBindingContract, GpuPreparedBindings and generated inputs.
    ☐ Validate active stages, vertex attributes/scalar interpretation/components/divisors, fragment output
      locations/scalar classes, resource layout, static state, target signature and capabilities.
    ☐ Add missing interface validation at preparation time; do not build parallel reflection/binding systems.
  ☐ Publish prepared objects only after successful validation.
    ☐ Retain needed dependency references without acquiring ownership of externally owned shader resources.
    ☐ Reject retired dependencies and obsolete executable/context generations.
    ☐ Invalidate on reload; revalidate incompatible changes before publishing replacements.
    ☐ Preserve existing deferred deletion; do not invent ResourceId for a managed aggregate.
    ☐ Use explicit prepared objects; defer registry/interning and bound future cache requirements in docs.
  ☐ Delegate tests for interface/format/sample mismatch, unsupported stages/settings, failed preparation,
    compatible/incompatible reload, explicit disposal and context generation replacement.
  ☐ Completion gate: failures cannot expose a partially usable pipeline; no steady-state interface queries.

Phase 5: Render passes and target lifetime
  Dependencies: Phases 1–4 target and state contracts.
  Outcome: explicit pass targets, routing, clears and store intentions with compatibility validation.

  ☐ Implement RenderPassDesc and target signature extraction/validation through existing FBO resources.
    ☐ Preserve output-location/draw-buffer/attachment-index distinctions and sparse routing.
    ☐ Supply wrapped/default target metadata through the chosen provider; reject unknown strict metadata.
    ☐ Retain borrowed references for the pass without changing FBO or attachment ownership.
    ☐ Recheck completeness/compatibility after replacement, resize or backing changes at safe boundaries.
    ☐ Reject mutation during an active pass or require end/rebegin; refresh viewport/render area after resize.
  ☐ Implement preserve, typed per-attachment clear and discard intentions where meaningful.
    ☐ Establish clear masks/scissor/render-area behavior independently of draw write masks.
    ☐ Preserve unrequested aspects; keep resolve/blit/readback/barriers explicit.
    ☐ End passes without disposing borrowed targets; clean up on setup failure.
  ☐ Delegate headless tests for sparse targets, packed depth/stencil aspects, typed clears, preservation,
    discard contract, metadata refresh, resize, replacement, format/sample changes and retirement.
  ☐ Completion gate: same-signature targets reuse a pipeline across dimensions/IDs, while incompatible
    or retired targets fail deterministically; clear behavior is independent of preceding draw state.

Phase 6: Immediate graphics submission
  Dependencies: Phases 3–5.
  Outcome: one authoritative entry point validates and establishes the full draw contract.

  ☐ Implement DynamicDrawState, geometry adapters and a thin GraphicsCommandContext.
    ☐ Support BeginPass, pipeline/dynamic selection, existing shader-input assignment, Draw and EndPass.
    ☐ Reject accidental nesting and draws outside a valid pass.
    ☐ Validate current target/generations, geometry layout/topology, required dynamics and resources.
    ☐ Use existing engine-aware shader activation and input publication, including CurrentShaderProgram,
      overlapping-owner restrictions, failure cleanup and recursion/mutation guards.
    ☐ Publish changed resources/uniforms even when the selected pipeline is unchanged.
    ☐ Establish all required state before issuing the draw; failure must emit no draw.
    ☐ Document and observe engine geometry helper mutations; prohibit untracked operations inside a pass.
    ☐ End/suspend authority at declared external boundaries and dirty affected categories before resuming.
  ☐ Delegate integration tests for missing dynamics/resources, invalid geometry, no-draw failures,
    changed inputs on the same pipeline, external operations, nesting rejection and exception cleanup.
  ☐ Completion gate: deterministic fixture draws use the full submission path with no hidden ambient
    state dependency or per-draw reflection/state queries under stable managed conditions.

Phase 7: Direct-lighting production migration
  Dependencies: Phases 1–6, including baseline evidence and first-consumer engine adapter.
  Outcome: first VGE production pass exercises the complete architecture.

  ☐ Migrate DirectLightingRenderer and its normal/isolated targets to prepared pipeline/pass submission.
    ☐ Preserve shader inputs, image formats, output routing, clear behavior and engine restoration.
    ☐ Preserve resource lifetimes, resize handling and readiness/failure behavior.
    ☐ Remove redundant local state setup only after the new owner covers it.
  ☐ Delegate deterministic old/reference-versus-migrated output tests and focused consumer regressions.
    ☐ Exercise first use, repeated draws, target resize, shader failure/reload and external state changes.
    ☐ Record state calls, steady-state queries and preparation/submission costs separately.
  ☐ Perform a second source review against the proposal and evidence; resolve discrepancies before
    migrating additional production consumers. Record remaining engine/live-runtime limitations.
  ☐ Completion gate: direct-lighting output and restoration are preserved, required state is explicit,
    and changed inputs are never skipped by redundant-state suppression.

Phase 8: Remaining VGE consumers and compatibility cleanup
  Dependencies: Phase 7 review gate; Phase 1 consumer inventory.
  Outcome: inventoried VGE passes adopt complete submission or have a documented scope disposition.

  ☐ Migrate composite and capture passes, then indexed-blend/MRT consumers.
    ☐ Extend per-consumer boundary adapters and metadata before migrating each caller.
    ☐ Move framebuffer blend policy to pipeline descriptions as its consumers migrate.
    ☐ Add output, lifetime, routing and restoration regression evidence for each consumer family.
  ☐ Migrate VGE-owned debug/tessellation/terrain-related draws using the required specialized adapters.
    ☐ Preserve engine-owned rendering behind explicit compatibility boundaries; do not claim ownership
      solely because a shader or draw helper is shared with VGE.
    ☐ Record any unconverted in-scope consumer as incomplete; require explicit scope agreement to defer it.
  ☐ Reconcile partial overrides and legacy helpers after inventorying remaining callers.
    ☐ Retain partial changes only outside complete submissions or through declared boundaries.
    ☐ Remove obsolete migrated blend storage/setup and temporary adapters only after caller/tests prove safety.
    ☐ Retain necessary capture/invalidation; remove individual scopes only with tracking/restoration evidence.
    ☐ Complete migrated type renames and references without unrelated hierarchy or ownership changes.
  ☐ Reconcile project.todo binding/hook items with the coverage matrix; do not mark unobserved paths done.
  ☐ Delegate focused consumer regressions and compute/shared-binding regressions affected by common changes.
  ☐ Completion gate: every inventoried consumer has an evidenced disposition, no migrated pass has two
    blend/state owners, and temporary compatibility code has been removed or assigned a justified purpose.

Phase 9: Acceptance evidence and measurements
  Dependencies: Phases 1–8.
  Outcome: implementation scope, correctness and measured costs are reviewable without overstated claims.

  ☐ Map each proposal section to implemented source and evidence in the implementation document.
    ☐ Cover naming, immutable identity, complete/partial semantics, all supported state, targets/lifetime,
      preparation, submission, engine boundaries and adoption; record limitations and follow-up exclusions.
  ☐ Delegate required Debug/Release builds and focused unit/headless integration suites.
    ☐ Consolidate prior valid receipts; rerun where later edits affect previously verified behavior.
    ☐ Record context, commands, pass/failure/skip counts and outstanding failures without hiding skips.
  ☐ Record comparable-workload state-call/query counts, CPU submission and preparation cost,
    prepared-object/cache retention, and representative GPU timing with instrumentation/environment details.
    ☐ Separate measured results from expected benefits; do not require an invented speedup threshold.
  ☐ Provide user-run live game/RenderDoc cases for migrated passes, engine handoff, resize and reload.
    ☐ Record supplied visual/runtime results separately from headless evidence; leave live acceptance
      pending if unavailable and do not launch the game to obtain it.
  ☐ Perform a final second review of implementation, contracts, remaining compatibility paths and evidence.
    ☐ Resolve verified issues and document explicit scope dispositions before completion claims.
  ☐ Final acceptance: required implementation and tests are complete, live evidence status is explicit,
    proposal requirements are accounted for, and no authority/performance claim exceeds verified coverage.

Open assumptions and follow-up boundaries
  Concrete API spellings are selected during design within the approved responsibility model.
  Whole-engine authority remains outside the initial scope; unknown engine/mod mutations retain fallback.
  Future interning needs bounded retention/eviction and equality checks for hash collisions.
  Prewarming/asynchronous preparation, render graphs and additional backends require separate plans.
  User-run runtime evidence can extend elapsed delivery time; test completion does not substitute for it.
